+# Scalar capture alias epochs: unqualified source candidate
+
+Base: `04bb082113ae6fc04eee4ea0c370cdf1f5b34648`. Branch:
+`perf/epoch-scalar-aliases-20260912`. No builds, tests, or benchmarks have run.
+This candidate is separate from outer validation reuse and owned output compaction.
+The root's preimplementation design follows; implementation details and pending
+qualification are recorded below it.
+
+The current scalar_moves::eliminate clears a register-sized aliases table at
+every basic block. Replace logical resets with a per-block generation while
+preserving source-version checks, writer invalidation, and all capture/dead-read
+and branch remapping logic. Keep this distinct from owned promotion output.
+
+A candidate representation is Option<(Reg, u32, NonZeroU32)> for source, source
+version and epoch. A NonZeroU32 niche can retain the current 12-byte size of
+Option<(Reg,u32)> on this host; check actual size and do not claim a portable
+layout guarantee. Avoid adding a second register-sized array. On a read, reuse
+an alias only when its epoch equals the current block and its source version is
+still current. On each write clear that destination alias as before. On capture
+record the current epoch. All decisions therefore see the same logical table.
+
+Use nonzero generations and handle wrapping explicitly: if the generation wraps
+to zero, clear aliases once and restart at one, so ancient aliases cannot become
+live again. This avoids new size limits or exceptional behavior even though the
+production caller currently caps code/registers at 100,000. No source version
+arithmetic changes. A tiny generation-advance helper can be tested at the wrap
+boundary without allocating billions of operations.
+
+Tests should retain the original algorithm for differential output/count
+comparisons across branches, loops, register writers, calls, and scalar widths;
+include a literal stale-capture counterexample at a block boundary and a direct
+wrap reset expectation. Preserve existing rejection/aliasing/overlap oracles.
+Measure only as a future distinct candidate with fixed fresh histories. This
+may lose on small functions because each alias lookup gains an epoch comparison;
+no speed claim follows from the asymptotic reset improvement alone.
+
+## Implemented scope
+
+`Alias` is `Option<(Reg, u32, NonZeroU32)>`: source register, source version,
+block epoch. There is one alias table as before and one scalar current epoch.
+A block start advances the epoch. A captured alias can rewrite a read only if
+both its epoch and its source version still match. Explicit writes still clear
+the destination alias and use the original `versions[r] += 1` arithmetic.
+Capture recognition, scalar widths and all operand rewrite order are unchanged.
+
+The initial nonzero epoch is one; the first block advances it to two. The initial
+table contains no aliases, so this numbering has no observable effect. Advancing
+`u32::MAX` clears the complete table before returning epoch one. No old entry can
+become current when epoch values are reused. This adds no code/register limit and
+uses no new unsafe code. The final read census, capture liveness, dead-operation
+selection and branch remapping are unchanged, including the original output copy
+loop. The separate owned-compaction candidate can compose at that final loop.
+
+## Prepared tests and retained reference
+
+`scalar_promote_moves_epoch_tests.rs` retains `eliminate` from
+`04bb082:crates/mir-export/src/lower/scalar_promote_moves.rs`. Only its
+name/visibility and the paths to unchanged parent helpers differ. The exact
+original function text, excluding trailing blank lines, has SHA-256
+`603fe733350f2f88137088676eba7914feed7407d75a85f350b82d5a5db9e7ff`.
+It keeps the original full `aliases.fill(None)` at every block boundary and is
+independent of the epoch helper and representation.
+
+Full serialized instruction streams and removal counts are compared on 512
+fixed-seed cases with branches, cycles, duplicate switch keys, capture casts,
+width/signedness changes, explicit writers, direct/indirect call operands and
+retained diagnostics. These are compiler-algorithm comparisons; the random
+streams are not executed or claimed as native semantic oracles.
+
+Literal checks cover all five scalar widths, including overwriting a captured
+source, and a join that bypasses capture without changing the source version.
+The latter must retain the original code and return the initial register zero
+instead of seven when run by the existing checked interpreter entrypoint.
+A direct generation-wrap check seeds both ancient epoch-one and maximum-epoch
+aliases, requires the table to clear before restarting at one, and checks that
+a normal advance does not clear physical entries. Existing semantic/aliasing/
+branch and instruction-budget fixtures remain unchanged.
+
+On aarch64 macOS, a prepared host-footprint test requires both the old alias slot
+and the epoch slot to occupy 12 bytes. This check has not run. The layout is not a
+portable guarantee or a measured memory improvement; qualification must reject
+or separately reconsider the representation if that host assertion fails.
+
+## Qualification still required
+
+Run the exporter debug/release tests and existing scalar-promotion/native/
+interpreter/JIT, aliasing and rejection fixtures under the shared lock. Root must
+also qualify any composition with owned output compaction against unchanged
+public bytecode and reports. Keep the same frozen VM/wrapper and launcher in all
+build-time comparisons, and freeze a new candidate/tool/source manifest before
+any independently predeclared screen. Preserve all earlier parked candidates.
+No speedup follows from removing table clears: small functions may lose to the
+additional epoch comparison on each alias read. No measurement is part of this
+commit or its source review.
