+# Move retained scalar-promotion bytecode into its output
+
+Unqualified candidate, recorded before production edits or measurements.
+Base: `04bb082`; owned branch `perf/owned-scalar-output-20260912` in
+`/Users/danluu/dev/rust-interp-build-plan-20260912`.
+
+The scalar promotion and scalar capture-removal passes already receive an
+owned mutable bytecode vector. Each finishes its analysis and early declines,
+allocates its replacement vector, then clones every retained operation. This
+duplicates owned diagnostic strings, switch tables and call argument vectors
+even though the old bytecode is discarded immediately after remapping.
+
+Change only the committed output loops to drain their original vectors and move
+unchanged operations into the output. Retain the original inspection match by
+borrowing each drained operation while deciding whether to rewrite it. Keep all
+candidate selection, writer/read proofs, scalar register numbering, immediate
+zero insertion, bounds and early returns before draining. Mapping must still
+record the output position for every original PC, including removed operations;
+both branch remapping passes and all report counters remain unchanged.
+
+Scalar capture forwarding mutates operands during its existing analysis; a
+zero-removal return must retain those mutations exactly as before. Draining is
+only after dead-capture classification and the zero-removal return. Removed
+operations are ordinary Rust values with owned fields; dropping them introduces
+no guest instruction or guest destructor. No additional optimization or
+invalidation strategy is introduced. The alias-table epoch proposal and
+redundant whole-program validation investigation remain separate.
+
+Tests will preserve the pre-change cloning loops as a test-only reference,
+compare serialized output/register counts/reports across existing scalar and
+move fixtures, and add literal control-flow/call/diagnostic checks. Retained
+nonempty heap allocations should survive the move path, proving that the
+candidate does not merely return equal cloned values. Early declines and paths
+with no dead captures must preserve their existing behavior. Existing execution
+fixtures retain native arithmetic/byte expectations and exact instruction-limit
+and fault checks; reference parity alone is not the semantic oracle.
+
+No tests, builds or benchmarks will be run by this agent. Root will qualify the
+commit serially under the shared lock and keep strict rustc checks, flags,
+bytecode equality requirements and the frozen VM unchanged. Current receipts
+put total scalar promotion near 52 ms for edited token builds, but they do not
+attribute that time to cloning and establish no speedup for this candidate.
+
+The implemented test-only reference algorithms come from the portions before
+the first `#[cfg(test)]` in `04bb082`'s scalar promotion and move-removal modules.
+Their original UTF-8 source SHA-256 digests are respectively
+`753ef109f8774d96535ac0da53fa35246c0a3abc136ba4e0afa39778dd36f1b4`
+and `32b7c28642d5e47dbcd223ae37eec99b219b96a59eec689454a44438ef1ed89d`.
+Only test module wiring and an elimination forwarding helper are added to those
+references. Literal expectations and existing execution oracles independently
+cover the behavior; reference equality checks preserve exact bytecode and
+report ordering.
+
+Independent source review found no blocker in the ownership transition, module
+wiring, remapping or literal fixtures. No correctness or performance execution
+has occurred for this candidate, so it remains unqualified.
