+# Reuse initialized local operand sizes
+
+Status: prospective, unqualified source candidate. No builds, tests or timings
+have been run for this change. This is separate from qualification of the four
+components in its base commit.
+
+Owned worktree: `/Users/danluu/dev/rust-interp-build-plan-20260912`.
+Branch: `perf/reuse-local-operand-sizes-20260912`.
+Base: `03d894adc4f1856a2c6c2b5a3f7a203a77c851e5`.
+
+## Scope and invariant
+
+`Lower::new` already normalizes each local type, obtains its layout, checks that
+it is sized and within the alignment limit, and stores the resulting byte size
+in its `Slot`. Its `local_types` entry records successful initialization for the
+same local and `Instance`. Frame packing can change offsets, but its independent
+layout checks preserve each local's size. No later lowering step changes those
+sizes. Reuse that size only for an unprojected `Copy` or `Move` operand whose
+`local_types` entry exists.
+
+Add a private `operand_size` helper, with the original normalized-type/layout
+query as its fallback. Use it only in scalar operand loading, ordinary aggregate
+field copies, and repeat element copies. These sites need a byte count rather
+than a complete layout. Keep every query at its existing evaluation position:
+scalar and field operands are evaluated first; repeat counts are evaluated
+first, followed by element size, operand evaluation, and the expansion bound.
+Do not change raw-pointer aggregate checks, calls, layout-bearing operations,
+scalar load width checks, or operand evaluation for zero-sized values.
+
+Projected places, constants, and `RuntimeChecks` continue through the original
+query. `Lower::empty` has no initialized `local_types`, so synthetic adapters and
+replay helpers retain the original fallback and errors even if they construct
+other storage. The optimization adds no persistent cache, flags or metadata, and
+does not change the scalar-layout certificate or its lifetime.
+
+## Correctness evidence to prepare
+
+Extend the existing native differential `local_layout_fixture.rs`, preserving
+its independent integer-only oracle and seed schedule. Add dynamic local values
+used by scalar operations and aggregate/repeat construction, including native
+128-bit values, aggregates larger than a scalar, and aligned zero-sized values.
+Retain projected reads and constants to exercise fallback behavior alongside
+initialized locals. Expected results must use native arithmetic rather than the
+interpreter or its bytecode helpers.
+
+The existing validator already hashes this fixture, checks native output count,
+and compares each result in both interpreter engines. Existing Result adapter,
+projection, runtime-check and malformed-input coverage remains relevant. Root
+will run the appropriate checks and compare candidate/base bytecode and reports
+before any timing. Source review will verify unchanged evaluation/error order,
+initialization and packing invariants, and synthetic fallback. No new tests
+should merely mirror the helper's condition without a real compiler context.
+
+## Performance boundary
+
+The avoided work is dispatch to an already-cached rustc layout query at these
+size-only sites. No hit rate or speedup is assumed. Root will use diagnostics
+and a separately fixed qualification plan to decide whether to measure or
+compose this candidate; existing four-component measurements remain separate.
+
+## Source preparation
+
+The implementation changes precisely the three planned query sites. A source
+audit found only initialization and scalar-frame packing write `Lower.locals`;
+only initialization fills `local_types`. The planner independently rejects any
+slot whose size differs from its original shape. The new helper does not depend
+on retaining `ChosenLayout` after capture consumes it.
+
+The extended fixture uses a non-inlined generic repeat helper for u8/u16/u32/u64,
+u128, a 32-byte `(u128, i128)` tuple, and an aligned ZST. Dynamic indexed reads
+consume repeated values; aggregate movement, constant repetition and an empty
+repeat are present alongside existing associated-type/projection cases. The
+native oracle derives low/high words arithmetically and uses literal 96-byte
+aggregate size and 64-byte marker alignment expectations. The signed product
+cannot overflow i128 for an i64 input. Source forms are not evidence of retained
+MIR opcode counts; qualification must inspect or compare the actual artifacts.
+
+The existing 111-input validator schedule and 261-input standalone fixture test
+are unchanged. Its source already appears in `inputs_sha256`, and native output
+count is asserted before comparing seeds. Compare baseline and candidate using
+this same extended fixture, rather than comparing changed fixture outputs with
+older fixtures. Existing synthetic Result adapter cases remain unchanged.
+
